# Checkwright's target-to-runner map — gate-sdk/SPEC.md §Layout and configuration.
# One `<target> <runner>` pair per live line; `#`-comments and blank lines are
# stripped, the same line grammar scripts/gates.list and native/targets.list use.
# Read through gate_native_runner (gate-sdk/lib/gate.sh) and never inlined at a
# call site.
#
# It is a RUNNER SELECTION and not a support commitment, which is why it is a
# separate file from native/targets.list rather than a second column on it: a
# mapping may name a platform the roster deliberately may not. What the
# separateness holds open is a PERMISSION and not a standing exception: it is
# what lets the next probed platform be built and measured BEFORE it may be
# declared, which is the whole point of the `native-artifacts` job. Read it off
# the permission rather than off whichever line happens to be off the roster —
# both darwin lines below are the attested path through that sequence, each
# mapped and built here while off the roster and each joined only once one run
# carried both halves of the predicate. A mapped triple that is also a roster
# line is that sequence finished, not the property lapsing.
#
# Two readers, one map. .github/workflows/publish.yml's roster job derives the
# release build matrix from it, and .github/workflows/gates.yml's
# `native-artifacts` job derives the CI producer's matrix from it. Each fails
# loudly on a target with no entry here rather than building somewhere wrong;
# the release refusal is the older of the two and its wording is unchanged by
# the move off an inline `declare -A`.
#
# The macOS labels are PROBED, not remembered: actions/runner-images' own
# available-images table, read 2026-09-08, carries no `macos-13` at all — the
# label a session working from memory reaches for first. The live GitHub-hosted
# x64 macOS labels there are `macos-15-intel` and `macos-26-intel`, beside the
# paid `-large` variants; `macos-latest` resolves to an arm64 image. Re-probe
# that table rather than trusting this paragraph if a leg ever fails to find a
# runner: an unmatched label does not fail fast, it queues.
#
# The x64 windows label needs no such probe and is the one line here attested by this
# repository's own runs: `windows-latest` is what both Windows install-smoke legs
# in .github/workflows/gates.yml have been running on for rounds, so it resolves
# to a live image by measurement rather than by a table read.
#
# The arm64-Linux label is PROBED on the same terms and read 2026-09-11 against
# that same available-images table: `ubuntu-24.04-arm` and `ubuntu-22.04-arm` are
# both Ready and `ubuntu-26.04-arm` is Preview, so the newest Ready label is the
# one taken. There is no `ubuntu-latest`-shaped label for this platform —
# `ubuntu-latest` resolves x64 — so unlike the Linux x64 line above this one
# carries a PINNED label that ages, which is a recurring cost and the same one
# both macOS lines already pay. Naming it here is what stops a later reader
# reading the pin as an oversight; the re-probe instruction above covers it.
#
# The arm64-Windows label is PROBED on the same terms, read 2026-09-24 off
# actions/runner-images' README: `windows-11-arm` is listed with no preview
# badge, and it is a pinned label that ages like the arm64-Linux one. Its image
# readme carries Git for Windows' bash and Rust, which is what the producer and
# the install-smoke leg on it run. Ride its Visual Studio 2026 default rather
# than pin the `windows-11-vs2026-arm` test label, whose lifetime nothing states:
# the first run whose leg on it logs the VS 2026 image is the first witness.
#
# Every `-latest` line here RIDES its image migrations, as do the hard-coded
# `ubuntu-latest` install-smoke-sh-linux leg: their subject is the platform an
# adopter meets today (.github/workflows/gates.yml's macOS leg header). The
# first witness of a migrated image is the first gates run whose leg logs it.
# Every other Linux job in .github/workflows/ builds and measures no adopter
# platform, so it PINS `ubuntu-24.04`: a `-latest` label moves it with no tree
# edit and, through a weeks-long rollout, varies run to run. Move those pins as
# one deliberate edit, whose own push is the new image's witness.
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu  ubuntu-latest
aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu ubuntu-24.04-arm
aarch64-apple-darwin      macos-latest
x86_64-apple-darwin       macos-15-intel
x86_64-pc-windows-msvc    windows-latest
aarch64-pc-windows-msvc   windows-11-arm
